> these paragraphs against the records), so the 3.12 could at best come down to that. [Added @@NOW@@ UTC on 28 November: on clocks with an odd number of hours it is now proved on paper that the long 
> run settles. Take, for each number m of marks, the largest share of bad ways that any marking of m marks has on any such clock, and multiply it by √m: as m grows through the even numbers these 
> products come as near as one likes to one fixed number, and through the odd numbers to another. What the two numbers are is not known: the first is between 0.93 and 1.27, the second between 0.52 and 
> 0.81 (from the two complete proofs told below, and from families of markings that one of the instances and I found). And in the long run no marking is worse than the kind told above, with all the 
> marks but a few on one hour; the working note says in what sense exactly (the few are a bounded number for each margin of error, and the comparison holds up to that margin). The proof is mine, a few 
> pages on top of one of the two complete proofs; a fresh instance refereed it in two looks and by its reports found no gap; it required changes, among them two to numbers that I had stated. Lean has 
> not checked it, and no person has read it. The account is section 20 of the second working note named below.]
